# From SŪTRADHĀRA — M0-T34 landed, and it corrects both D-26 and my own escalation

The operator-surface fix is in (`344254c64`). `ka_graha_sancara` now renders `service_down`
instead of `service_ok`; exactly one of the eight assets changes, and a test asserts that count.
`service_down` was not invented — it already existed in `plan.ts`'s `AssetState` union outside
`READY_STATES`, was already styled in the blocked-build modal and already mapped to `failed` in
the node renderer. Only the cockpit's union lagged.

Two corrections it made rather than working around. Both matter more than the fix.

## 1 — The causal claim in D-26 is partly wrong, and mine was too

D-26 says the green satisfies downstream build prerequisites via `READY_STATES`. **It does not,
because `service_ok` is unstorable**: `asset_throughput.state`'s CHECK allows only
`dormant|building|lit|stale|error|incomplete`, and zero rows hold `service_ok`. `deriveState`'s
only consumer is `cockpit/stats/route.ts`. **So the fix closes the operator-surface H4 and leaves
the build-gate one untouched** — the build path is satisfied by `asset_throughput.state = 'lit'`
instead.

I passed D-26's reading of `plan.ts:196`/`:250` on to the KĀRAKA as established. It checked
rather than inherited, and found the two halves are different defects with different mechanisms.
The fix is real but narrower than either of us said, and I would rather that be on the record than
have the campaign believe the build gate was closed today.

**And it found the actual build-gate root cause, which is worse than the serving one.**
`ka_graha_sancara.py:82` computes `status = "success" if health == "healthy" else "error"` — and
**never uses it.** `WriterResult` has nowhere to carry it. So the writer ran its own probe, got
`unhealthy`, wrote that verdict to the registry, computed the correct status from it — and the
orchestrator lit the asset **32 milliseconds later**, because nothing in the contract could carry
the answer from the writer to the promotion.

That is the same shape as `has_substeps`, one layer further in: a detector that runs, produces the
right answer, and has no channel to the thing that decides. The KĀRAKA correctly did **not** fix
it — carrying a status out of a writer is a change to the FROZEN writer contract, which is **P3**,
reserved. It is filed as its F-1 and I have queued nothing.

## 2 — My "healthy with no probe" claim was wrong

I told you three assets read `healthy` with `health_probe` NULL and that "no probe could ever have
produced the word." **That was wrong.** `health_probe` is a probe **specification**, not a verdict.
The verdict lives in `service_health` + `last_selftest_at` + `selftest_detail` — and all three
`healthy` values are **earned**, with real self-test records behind them.

The correct figure: the never-probed bucket is **four**, not five, and it **includes both `bg_*`
assets** — which D-26 and I both credited with real probes on the strength of a non-null
`health_probe`. We each read a specification column as evidence of a verdict, which is the same
error the campaign keeps cataloguing, made by the two agents cataloguing it.

## One more, and it connects to R0's headline defect

`bg_ephemeris_engine` has sat in `error` since **2026-06-18** with a real diagnosis recorded — a
damaged Swiss Ephemeris file — that nobody acted on. That is the 66-day red the plan names as
R0's very first P0, and the KĀRAKA notes it is plausibly the **same root cause** as
`ka_graha_sancara`'s failure. R0 territory, untouched, filed as F-4.

If that connection holds, R0's first repair and R3's service probe share a cause, and finding that
out before R0 opens is worth more than it cost.

## Also worth recording

The KĀRAKA pinned M3's half of your ruling with an explicitly `M3-DEFERRED` **test**, so a
never-probed service continuing to render `service_ok` is now held by an assertion rather than by
a comment — M3 has to change it on purpose. One of its three mutations was specifically designed
to pre-decide M3's question, and killed the test. That boundary is test-held, not prose-held.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
